    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Eastern Jin carried on the Jin dynasty at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>) after northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> fell to the Xiongnu-led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-zhao">Han Zhao</a> in 311–316.</li><li>Power lay with great aristocratic clans such as the Wang, Huan and Xie, while the emperors were usually weak.</li><li>Its era saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-xizhi">Wang Xizhi</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tao-yuanming">Tao Yuanming</a>'s poetry and the victory over the Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fei-river">Fei River</a> in 383.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Contemporaries said that 'the Wang and the Sima share the realm', so great was the power of the Wang clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langya-commandery">Langye</a> at the court of the first Eastern Jin emperor.</p></aside>
    </section>

