    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yuan-of-jin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-yuan-of-jin.jpg" alt="Emperor Yuan of Jin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yuan-of-jin">Emperor Yuan of Jin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sima_Rui_(Jin_Yuandi).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Eastern Jin was the continuation, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-jin-dynasty">Western Jin dynasty</a> founded by the Sima family in 266. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-eight-princes">War of the Eight Princes</a> (291–306) wrecked the Western Jin, the Xiongnu-led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-zhao">Han Zhao</a> state sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 311, capturing Emperor Huai, and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 316, capturing Emperor Min. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yuan-of-jin">Sima Rui</a>, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langya-commandery">Langye</a>, had been posted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jianye</a> in 307 on the advice of the aristocrat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-dao">Wang Dao</a>, and had won the cooperation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> families of the lower Yangtze. In 317 he proclaimed himself King of Jin, and in 318, after news of Emperor Min's death, he took the imperial title at Jiankang (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>), as Jianye had been renamed in 313; he is known as Emperor Yuan. The Wang clan of Langye, led by Wang Dao at court and his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-dun">Wang Dun</a> in command of the middle Yangtze, was so dominant that contemporaries said 'the Wang and the Sima share the realm'. Hundreds of thousands of northern refugees, from great aristocratic lineages to peasant families, followed the court south in successive waves, settling around Jiankang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jingkou">Jingkou</a> and the middle Yangtze. The new regime rested on an uneasy partnership between émigré northern clans, the established southern gentry and a weak imperial house.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jiankang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jiankang.jpg" alt="Jiankang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Southern_Dynasties_map_of_Nanjing.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">&quot;Jinling Ancient and Modern Map Research&quot; written by Chen Yi in the Ming Dynasty., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The migrations transformed southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Northern émigrés were at first entered on separate 'white registers' and settled in 'lodged' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> and counties named after their abandoned homes in the north, which kept them exempt from regular taxes; repeated campaigns of <em>tuduan</em>, 'registration by actual residence', such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-wen">Huan Wen</a>'s of 364, sought to bring them onto the ordinary tax rolls. Great families, both northern and southern, built up extensive estates in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> delta and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>, worked by tenants and dependants, and cleared new land in hills and wetlands, laying foundations for the later economic dominance of the south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a> farming, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, salt and river commerce sustained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a>, which grew into a great city, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> handled maritime trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and beyond. Intellectual life was led by aristocrats devoted to <em>qingtan</em>, 'pure conversation' on metaphysical questions drawn from the <em>Laozi</em>, <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuangzi">Zhuangzi</a></em> and <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yijing">Yijing</a></em>, as recorded in the later <em>Shishuo xinyu</em>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> spread among the elite through monks such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhi-dun">Zhi Dun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huiyuan">Huiyuan</a>, whose community on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lu">Mount Lu</a> became a major centre, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Daoism</a> flourished in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangqing">Shangqing</a> revelations received near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mao">Maoshan</a> in the 360s and in the alchemical writings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ge-hong">Ge Hong</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fei-river"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-fei-river.jpg" alt="Battle of Fei River" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fei-river">Battle of Fei River</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Fei_River.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SY, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Eastern Jin never had a large standing army under direct court control. Military power lay with regional commanders, above all in the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> province of Jing, whose governors repeatedly challenged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-dun">Wang Dun</a> rebelled in 322 and 324, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/su-jun">Su Jun</a>'s rebellion of 327–329 captured the capital. The court nevertheless launched several northern expeditions. Zu Ti recovered much of the land south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> in the 310s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-wen">Huan Wen</a>, the dominant general of the mid-century, conquered the Cheng Han state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> in 347, reached the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> against the Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> in 354 and retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 356, but was badly defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fangtou">Fangtou</a> in 369. In 377 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xie-xuan">Xie Xuan</a> formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beifu-army">Beifu</a>, or 'Northern Headquarters', army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jingkou">Jingkou</a> from hardy northern refugees. When Fu Jian of the Former Qin invaded with a vast army in 383, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xie-shi">Xie Shi</a> and Xie Xuan, under the overall direction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xie-an">Xie An</a>, defeated its advance forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fei-river">Battle of Fei River</a>, and the Former Qin collapsed. The Beifu army later produced the generals who decided the dynasty's fate, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-laozhi">Liu Laozhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liu-song">Liu Yu</a>, whose campaigns conquered the Southern Yan in 410, Western Shu in 413 and the Later Qin in 417, briefly recovering Chang'an and Luoyang.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/huan-wen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huan-wen.jpg" alt="Huan Wen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/huan-wen">Huan Wen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huan_Wen_%E6%A1%93%E6%BA%AB.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Eastern Jin emperors reigned more than they ruled. Many came to the throne as children or were dominated by ministers, and effective government passed between great clans: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-clan-of-langya">Wang of Langye</a> under Emperor Yuan and his successors, the Yu family of the empress dowager in the 320s and 330s, the Huan of Qiao under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-wen">Huan Wen</a>, who deposed Emperor Fei in 371 and was prevented from usurping the throne only by his death in 373, and the Xie of Chenjun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xie-an">Xie An</a>. The central institutions of the Western Jin were preserved in form, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/department-of-state-affairs">Department of State Affairs</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-secretariat">Secretariat</a> and the Nine Ranks system of official recruitment, through which local 'impartial judges' graded candidates. In practice the ranks reflected family pedigree, entrenching an aristocracy that monopolised high office and married within its own circle. Provincial administration was dominated by the governors of Yang province around the capital and Jing province upstream, whose rivalry shaped politics throughout the century. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xiaowu-of-northern-wei">Xiaowu</a> (r. 372–396), after Xie An's death in 385, tried to restore imperial power with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-daozi">Sima Daozi</a>, but their quarrels, and Daozi's control of government under the disabled Emperor An, weakened the throne further and opened the way to the military strongmen of the dynasty's last decades.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lantingji-xu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lantingji-xu.jpg" alt="Lantingji Xu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lantingji-xu">Lantingji Xu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:XingshuLantingxv.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Eastern Jin preserved the legitimacy, institutions and high culture of the Han and Jin world in the south while northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was divided among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a>, and in doing so began the long shift of China's population and economic centre towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> remained the capital of the Southern Dynasties, the Liu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, Southern Qi, Liang and Chen, which inherited Eastern Jin institutions and aristocratic society until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> conquest of 589. Its cultural achievement was outstanding. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-xizhi">Wang Xizhi</a>, whose preface to the poems composed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lantingji-xu">Orchid Pavilion</a> gathering of 353 became the most celebrated work of Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>, was revered as the 'sage of calligraphy'; the painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gu-kaizhi">Gu Kaizhi</a>, to whom the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/admonitions-of-the-instructress-to-the-court-ladies">Admonitions of the Instructress to the Court Ladies</a></em> scroll is attributed, was among the first Chinese artists known by name; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tao-yuanming">Tao Yuanming</a>'s poems of rural retreat became models for later Chinese poetry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> found an intellectual footing among the elite. The pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a>, who set out from the north in 399, returned by sea in 412 and later translated texts at Jiankang, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huiyuan">Huiyuan</a>'s treatise arguing that monks need not bow to rulers framed a lasting question in Chinese politics. Neighbours took note: Baekje and Goguryeo sent embassies, and Buddhism reached Baekje from the Eastern Jin in 384.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han.jpg" alt="Emperor Wu of Han" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%BC%A2%E6%AD%A6%E5%B8%9D.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The final decades of the dynasty were marked by rebellion and usurpation. In 399 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-en">Sun En</a>, a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/way-of-the-five-pecks-of-rice">Five Pecks of Rice</a> Daoist movement, rose in the coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>, where the court's conscription of dependants had caused deep resentment; his followers devastated the region before he was defeated and drowned himself in 402, and his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-xun-rebel">Lu Xun</a> carried on the revolt until 411. Taking advantage of the chaos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-xuan">Huan Xuan</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-wen">Huan Wen</a> and master of Jing province, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> in 402 and at the end of 403 forced Emperor An to abdicate, founding his own short-lived Chu dynasty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liu-song">Liu Yu</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beifu-army">Beifu</a> officer of humble origin, led a rising against him in 404, restored Emperor An and became the real ruler of the state. His victories over Lu Xun and over the northern states of Southern Yan and Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> brought him unrivalled prestige, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> was lost again to the Xia in 418. Early in 419 Liu Yu had Emperor An strangled and placed his brother on the throne as Emperor Gong, who abdicated to him in 420. Liu Yu founded the Liu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>, and the deposed emperor was murdered in 421, ending the Sima house.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Eastern Jin carried the Jin dynasty south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> after the fall of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, preserved its institutions and aristocratic culture at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a>, repelled the Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fei-river">Fei River</a> in 383, and fostered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>, poetry and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> that shaped Chinese civilisation for centuries.</em></p>

